{"candidates":[{"analogy":{"adopted_commitment_refs":[],"disanalogies":["In a biological monoculture, convergence is often enforced by centralized agricultural markets; in software, it can occur simply because one library is objectively better, an emergent phenomenon without a central enforcer.","Software vulnerabilities can be patched globally once discovered, whereas biological vulnerabilities require physical replacement of the crop.","Software systems can be partially diverse at different layers (e.g., different apps running on the same OS), adding nuance to what 'monoculture' means."],"overturn_conditions":["Empirical data shows that decentralized operators naturally maintain high stack diversity without external regulation.","The primary failure mode in decentralized networks is found to be local resource exhaustion, not upstream dependency compromise.","The shared-cause failure rate in the decentralized network is proven to be lower than the internal coordination failure rate of the large monolith."],"shared_structure":["Both decentralized networks and agricultural monocultures have the appearance of diversity at the operator level (different farmers/independent services) but hide a fatal lack of diversity at the dependency level (identical seed strain/identical library).","In both systems, a shared environmental shock (a specific pathogen / a specific software vulnerability) bypasses the 'redundancy' of having many independent units because the units are functionally identical in the vulnerable dimension.","Correlated failure is caused by an unmanaged, hidden common cause that emerges from independent actors converging on the same optimal solution."],"source_memory_refs":["SCR_002"],"transfer_claims":["Just as the Irish Potato Famine demonstrated that many independent farms were not resilient to a single disease because they shared a single potato variety, a decentralized network of services is not resilient if they all rely on a single common library (e.g., log4j or OpenSSL).","The 'anti-resilience' observed in the network (underinvestment in diverse stacks due to cost) is analogous to the economic pressures that drove farmers to adopt the single most productive potato variety, externalizing the risk of rare correlated failure."]},"checker_specs":[],"claim":"A decentralized network can only be said to be truly independent if the operators have heterogenous dependencies (different cloud providers, library stacks, and supply chains); if they converge on identical tools, the system is vulnerable to a monoculture collapse where a single software vulnerability acts as a shared cause.","counterconditions":["The operators use provably heterogeneous technology stacks with disjoint dependencies","Failures are shown to be triggered by local, operator-specific misconfigurations rather than shared upstream software defects","The large monolith uses identical internal components, meaning its redundancy is purely additive but functionally non-diverse"],"evidence_refs":[],"mechanism":"Independence in distributed systems is not guaranteed by organizational boundaries. If operators independently optimize for cost and reliability, they will naturally adopt the same industry-standard tools (e.g., a specific cloud provider, OS, or cryptographic library). This convergent evolution creates a hidden shared cause. A single vulnerability in that common dependency will trigger simultaneous failures across the decentralized network, making it strictly less resilient than the large monolith, which at least internalizes the cost of its single stack and can patch its internal redundancy in a coordinated manner.","optional_refs":[],"sidecar":{"requested_context_aliases":["ba687c5f145e404c53a84549cf366e0b6e0106eec67404c07231357fed09e672","09a3c8106b138005252dd72e7642b00f65aa891fecff6e3fa95b043a5b98435b"],"search_signal":"productive"},"typicality":0.85}],"simulation_proposals":[]}